== To the Reader

Christian reader, since the epistle dedicatory has run so long, as the occasion required, I will do little more than give you the grounds and reasons for sending this little piece out into the world, especially in such a day as this. Now, my reasons are these:

#emph[First], because God by His present dealings calls more loudly for closet prayer now than He has done in the last twenty years that have now passed over our heads. See more of this in the 16th argument for closet prayer.

#emph[Secondly], because I have various reasons to fear that many Christians do not clearly or fully understand the necessity, excellence, and usefulness of this subject; that many—oh, that I could not say any—live in too great a neglect of this indispensable duty; and that more than a few, for lack of light, err in the very practice of it.

#emph[Thirdly], for the refreshing, support, and encouragement of all those churches of Christ that walk in the fear of the Lord and in the comfort of the Holy Spirit, especially that particular church to which I am joined.

#emph[Fourthly], to preserve and keep up the power of religion and godliness in men’s houses, hearts, and lives. The power of religion and godliness lives, thrives, or dies as closet prayer lives, thrives, or dies. Godliness never rises to a higher pitch than when men keep closest to their closets.

#emph[Fifthly], because closet prayer is a most sovereign remedy, a most precious antidote of God’s own prescribing, against the plague that is now raging in the midst of us (1 Kgs 8:37–39).

#emph[Sixthly], because every man is really what he is secretly. Never tell me how handsomely, how neatly, how finely this or that man acts his part before others; but tell me, if you can, how he acts his part before God in his closet, for the man is certainly what he is #emph[secretly]. There are many who sweat on the stage who are stone-cold in their closets.

#emph[Seventhly], though many worthy men have written worthily on all the other parts of prayer, yet none, of earlier or later date, that have come under my eye have written any treatise on this subject. I have wondered not a little that so many eminent writers should pass over this great and princely duty of closet prayer, either with a few brief touches, or else in a very great silence. If you consult various bodies of theology, you will find that all they say clearly and distinctly about closet prayer may be brought into a very narrow compass, if not into a nutshell. I have also asked various old disciples whether they have ever heard one sermon on closet prayer among all the thousand sermons they have heard in their days, and they have answered, “No.” I have also asked them whether they had ever read any treatise on the subject, and they have answered, “No.” And truly this has been no small encouragement to me to offer my mite. And if this small attempt of mine is so blessed as to provoke others who have better heads, and hearts, and hands than any I have, to do Christ and His people more service by handling this choice point more fully than I have been able to reach, I will rejoice in that.

#emph[Eighthly] and lastly, the favor, the good acceptance, and the fair treatment that my other poor labors have found, not only in this nation, but in other countries also, has moved me to put pen to paper once more. And I hope that “the favor of Him who dwelt in the burning bush” (Deut 33:16) will rest on this, as it has, to the glory of free grace, rested on my former endeavors. I could add other reasons, but let these suffice.

Good reader, when you are in your closet, pray hard for a poor, weak, worthless worm, that I may be found faithful and fruitful until death, so that at last I may receive a crown of life. So, wishing you all happiness both in this lower world and in that upper world, I remain,

#v(1em)

#align(right)[Yours in our dear Lord Jesus,]

#align(right)[#emph[Thomas Brooks].]
